./workflow_tests/README.md-30-## Limitations
./workflow_tests/README.md-31-Not all workflows can always be tested this way, for example:
./workflow_tests/README.md-32-- Act and Act-js do not support all the runner types available in GitHub Actions, like `macOS` runners or some specific version of `Ubuntu` runners like `ubuntu-20.04-64core`. In these cases the job will be omitted entirely and cannot be tested
./workflow_tests/README.md-33-- Testing more complex workflows in their entirety can be extremely time-consuming and cumbersome. It is often optimal to mock most of the steps with expressions printing the input and output conditions
./workflow_tests/README.md:34:- Due to the way `Act` and `Act-js` handle workflow output, not much can be checked in the test. What is available, namely whether the job/step executed or not, whether it was successful or not and what its printed output was, should be enough in most scenarios
./workflow_tests/README.md-35-- `Act` does not seem to support the conditions set on event parameters when determining whether to run the workflow or not, namely for a workflow defined with:
./workflow_tests/README.md-36-```yaml
./workflow_tests/README.md-37-on:
./workflow_tests/README.md-38-  pull_request:
./workflow_tests/README.md-39-    types: [opened, edited, reopened]
./workflow_tests/README.md-40-```
./workflow_tests/README.md-41-running `act pull_request -e event_data.json` with `event_data.json` having `{"action": "opened"}` will execute the workflow (as expected), running for example `act push` will not execute it (as expected), but running `act pull_request -e event_data.json` with `event_data.json` having for example `{"action": "assigned"}` **will still execute the workflow** even though it should only be executed with `action` being `opened`, `edited` or `reopened`. This only applies to running the workflow with `Act`, in the GitHub environment it still works as expected
