./README.md-182-# Debugging
./README.md-183-### iOS
./README.md-184-1. If running on the iOS simulator pressing `⌘D` will open the debugging menu.
./README.md-185-2. This will allow you to attach a debugger in your IDE, React Developer Tools, or your browser.
./README.md-186-3. For more information on how to attach a debugger, see [React Native Debugging Documentation](https://reactnative.dev/docs/debugging#chrome-developer-tools)
./README.md-187-
./README.md-188-Alternatively, you can also set up debugger using [Flipper](https://fbflipper.com/). After installation, press `⌘D` and select "Open Debugger". This will open Flipper window. To view data stored by Onyx, go to Plugin Manager and install `async-storage` plugin.
./README.md-189-
./README.md-190-## Android
./README.md-191-Our React Native Android app now uses the `Hermes` JS engine which requires your browser for remote debugging. These instructions are specific to Chrome since that's what the Hermes documentation provided.
./README.md-192-1. Navigate to `chrome://inspect`
./README.md-193-2. Use the `Configure...` button to add the Metro server address (typically `localhost:8081`, check your `Metro` output)
./README.md-194-3. You should now see a "Hermes React Native" target with an "inspect" link which can be used to bring up a debugger. If you don't see the "inspect" link, make sure the Metro server is running
./README.md-195-4. You can now use the Chrome debug tools. See [React Native Debugging Hermes](https://reactnative.dev/docs/hermes#debugging-hermes-using-google-chromes-devtools)
./README.md-196-
./README.md-197-## Web
./README.md-198-
./README.md:199:To make it easier to test things in web, we expose the Onyx object to the window, so you can easily do `Onyx.set('bla', 1)`.
